=== meta
title   = "Coding workflow"
profile = "geml-statechart/v1"
===

%% A workflow for writing code. Read it as four
%% claims about what an agent may do while it works on your repository:
%%
%%   1. it cannot edit a file before it has written down a plan;
%%   2. it cannot run the command while it is editing;
%%   3. it cannot say it is done without a recorded test result;
%%   4. the last step is a person's, not the model's.
%%
%% The states name uses (edit, test); tools.geml beside this file binds them to
%% this project's tools, so the same workflow runs on every host.

=== diagram {#flow format=geml-statechart caption="Coding workflow"}
{
  "initial": "explore",

  "states": {
    "explore": {
      "title": "Explore",
      "description": "Read the code before proposing anything; record goal and touched, then move to plan.",
      "prompt": "#explore"
    },
    "plan": {
      "title": "Plan",
      "description": "Write the plan down before touching a file: plan is the steps, command the one command that decides whether the change works.",
      "prompt": "#plan"
    },
    "implement": {
      "title": "Implement",
      "description": "Make the change. Editing is possible here and nowhere else; to run the command, move to verify.",
      "prompt": "#implement",
      "rules": ["edit"]
    },
    "verify": {
      "title": "Verify",
      "description": "Run the command; its exit status is the evidence. If it failed, go back and fix it.",
      "prompt": "#verify",
      "rules": ["test"]
    },
    "review": {
      "title": "Review",
      "description": "Hand back to the person: what changed, what the command said, what you are unsure about.",
      "prompt": "#review"
    },
    "done": { "title": "Done", "description": "Finished. Say in one line what shipped.", "final": true }
  },

  "vars": {
    "goal":       { "default": "", "note": "What this change is supposed to achieve, in one sentence" },
    "touched":    { "default": "", "note": "The files this change edits, space separated" },
    "plan":       { "default": "", "note": "The steps, written before any file is touched" },
    "command":    { "default": "", "note": "The command that decides whether this works, e.g. npm test" },
    "tests-pass": { "default": false, "source": { "tool": "test" } }
  },

  "transitions": [
    { "from": "explore", "to": "plan", "guard": "goal and touched are recorded" },
    { "from": "plan", "to": "implement", "guard": "plan and command are recorded" },
    { "from": "implement", "to": "verify", "note": "The edit is made; go run the command." },
    { "from": "verify", "to": "implement",
      "guard": "the command failed, or the change is not finished",
      "actions": [ { "reset": ["tests-pass"] } ],
      "note": "Go back and edit; the last run's evidence is cleared." },
    { "from": "verify", "to": "review",
      "guard": { "properties": { "tests-pass": { "const": true } } },
      "note": "The command passed. Hand it to a person." },
    { "from": "review", "to": "done", "approval": true,
      "note": "The person approved it: a human says yes at the moment it is taken, not a flag recorded earlier." },
    { "from": "review", "to": "implement",
      "guard": "the person asked for changes",
      "actions": [ { "reset": ["tests-pass"] } ] }
  ]
}
===

## Explore {#explore}

Read the code before proposing anything. You can read, grep, find and list —
nothing here edits the repository, so there is no way to change it while you
are still working out what is in it.

Record `goal` in one sentence, and `touched` with the files you expect to
change. When both are recorded you may move to #plan.

## Plan {#plan}

Write the plan down before touching a file. `plan` is the steps; `command` is
the one command that will decide whether the change works — the project's test
command, not a guess.

You still cannot edit anything from here. That is the point: the plan exists
before the diff does.

## Implement {#implement}

Make the change. Editing exists here and nowhere else in this workflow, and
the test command is not yours to run here — if you want to run it, move to
#verify, which is a recorded transition rather than a quiet decision.

## Verify {#verify}

Run `command`. Its exit status is the evidence: `tests-pass` is written from
the real exit code, never from optimism, and the journal shows every run.

If it failed, go back to #implement and fix it. The round trip is recorded
either way, so "we tried three times" is a fact rather than a memory.

## Review {#review}

Hand back to the person. Summarise what changed, what the command said, and
what you are unsure about. Nothing else happens until they answer.

## Why these four states {#why}

The gates are worth the ceremony only where a wrong action is expensive and the
right order is knowable in advance. Editing before planning, and declaring
victory without running anything, are both cheap to prevent and expensive to
discover later — so they are worth a state each.

Anything finer than this — a state per file, a state per function — is the
failure mode, not the goal: it spends the model's judgement on paperwork. If a
step's order does not matter, it does not deserve a state.
